Now I understand the task. Let me analyze the requirements:

1. Implement `listTools` and `callTool` in `server.mjs`
2. Expose `note_create` with required non-empty `title` and `body`
3. Expose `note_get` with required `note_id`
4. Store notes in memory
5. Return structured text content
6. Use stable generated ids
7. Reject invalid inputs or unknown tools
8. Set `additionalProperties: false` on every tool input (from skill description)

Let me look at the tests to understand what's expected:
- `create then get`: Creates a note with title 'A' and body 'B', gets an id from the response (matches `n\d+`), then gets the note with that id and checks the title is in the text.
- `lists two tools`: Checks that `listTools()` returns 2 tools.

From the test, the id must match `n\d+` - so it should be `n` followed by digits.

Let me think about the MCP tool schema. The MCP protocol uses JSON Schema for tool input schemas. Each tool should have:
- name
- description
- inputSchema with type: 'object', properties, required, and additionalProperties: false

For `note_create`:
- title: required, non-empty string
- body: required, non-empty string

For `note_get`:
- note_id: required string

Let me think about "stable generated ids". The test creates a note and then extracts the id with `/n\d+/`. So the id format is `n` followed by digits. To make it stable, I could use an incrementing counter. Let me use a module-level counter starting at 1, so ids are `n1`, `n2`, etc.

Actually, "stable generated ids" - this might mean that ids are deterministic rather than random. An incrementing counter is stable in the sense that it's predictable. Let me go with an incrementing counter.

For the response format, MCP tools/call returns:
```json
{
  "content": [
    {"type": "text", "text": "..."}
  ]
}
```

The test accesses `c.content[0].text`, so the response should have a `content` array with a text object.

Let me look at the protocol.mjs again - when `callTool` throws an error, the protocol catches it and returns an error. But the test calls `callTool` directly, so if it throws, the test fails. For invalid inputs, we should throw errors.

Let me write the server.mjs:

```javascript
const notes = new Map();
let nextId = 1;

export async function listTools() {
  return [
    {
      name: 'note_create',
      description: 'Create a new note with a title and body.',
      inputSchema: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          title: { type: 'string', description: 'The title of the note.' },
          body: { type: 'string', description: 'The body of the note.' }
        },
        required: ['title', 'body'],
        additionalProperties: false
      }
    },
    {
      name: 'note_get',
      description: 'Get a note by its id.',
      inputSchema: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          note_id: { type: 'string', description: 'The id of the note to retrieve.' }
        },
        required: ['note_id'],
        additionalProperties: false
      }
    }
  ];
}

function isNonEmptyString(value) {
  return typeof value === 'string' && value.length > 0;
}

export async function callTool(name, args) {
  if (name === 'note_create') {
    const { title, body } = args || {};
    if (!isNonEmptyString(title)) {
      throw new Error('title is required and must be a non-empty string');
    }
    if (!isNonEmptyString(body)) {
      throw new Error('body is required and must be a non-empty string');
    }
    const id = `n${nextId++}`;
    notes.set(id, { id, title, body });
    return {
      content: [{ type: 'text', text: `Created note ${id}: ${title}` }]
    };
  }
  if (name === 'note_get') {
    const { note_id } = args || {};
    if (!isNonEmptyString(note_id)) {
      throw new Error('note_id is required and must be a non-empty string');
    }
    const note = notes.get(note_id);
    if (!note) {
      throw new Error(`note not found: ${note_id}`);
    }
    return {
      content: [{ type: 'text', text: `${note.title}\n\n${note.body}` }]
    };
  }
  throw new Error(`unknown tool: ${name}`);
}
```

